+### NeRF Network Architecture with Appearance Code
+An extension of the original NeRF network architecture that adds a low-dimensional appearance code to handle illumination changes [S2, unofficial]. The appearance code is 16-dimensional, added to the second last layer of the NeRF network $F_\phi$, and is individually stored and optimized for every frame [S2, unofficial]. NeuralBody baselines use 128-dimensional codes, whereas A-NeRF uses 16-dimensional codes to help learning global effects without deteriorating the benefits of relative encoding [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Training takes approximately three days on 8 Tesla V100 GPUs for 25,000 images [S1, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
-- **Strengths:** Handles illumination changes effectively; generates high-resolution images (up to 1024x1024) [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of appearance code dimensionality [S2, unofficial].
-- **When to use:** When high-resolution, style-controlled 3D-aware synthesis is needed [S1, unofficial].
-- **When not to use:** When computational resources are limited or real-time inference is required [S1, unofficial].
